WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.360
Enterprises should treat Windows telemetry identity as a governance topic, not just a workstation setting.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:21.774
The organization needs to know which diagnostic level is configured, which endpoints are allowed, which services depend on telemetry, which reporting products consume the data, and who is accountable for explaining that posture to users, auditors, legal counsel, and security teams.

00:00:21.774 --> 00:00:32.602
Microsoft documentation describes policy paths for managing diagnostic data through Group Policy and MDM, including AllowTelemetry, dump collection limits, and diagnostic log collection limits.

00:00:32.602 --> 00:00:44.131
It also describes Windows diagnostic data processor configuration for eligible Microsoft Entra joined devices, where Microsoft may act as processor and the organization as controller under defined conditions.

00:00:44.131 --> 00:00:52.093
That governance distinction matters for data subject requests, retention, and compliance evidence. A privacy baseline should document purpose.

00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:59.991
If Delivery Optimization reporting is enabled, say why. If optional diagnostic data is allowed for a pilot group, define the scope and review date.

00:00:59.991 --> 00:01:06.806
If Feedback Hub or app telemetry is permitted, explain how feedback data differs from Windows diagnostic processor controls.

00:01:06.806 --> 00:01:14.386
Vague collection is hard to defend; bounded collection is easier to explain. Security teams also need to understand lawful disclosure risk.

00:01:14.386 --> 00:01:22.985
A persistent device identifier may become meaningful during an investigation when combined with provider records, account logs, IP history, and timestamps.

00:01:22.985 --> 00:01:29.482
That can support incident response and abuse investigations, but it also raises user trust and transparency questions.

00:01:29.482 --> 00:01:34.577
Organizations should not promise anonymity where managed devices generate service records.

00:01:34.577 --> 00:01:44.705
Effective governance is visible in artifacts: policy settings, management baselines, data-flow notes, retention schedules, user notices, exception registers, and periodic reviews.

00:01:44.705 --> 00:01:48.590
The objective is not to remove every Microsoft connection from Windows.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:57.380
It is to make identity and telemetry choices deliberate, documented, minimized, monitored, and proportionate to the organization’s risk and operational needs.
